            <section class="mt-content-container">
                <p>The classical description of spectroscopy begins with the observations that (a) atoms and molecules are composed of charged particles and (b) an electromagnetic field can interact with the atom or molecule by exerting a force on these charges. The size and influence of the force exerted on the molecules depends on the form of the potential binding the charges together, the magnitude of the charges, and the strength of the external field. The simplest model needs to account for the light, the charged particles that constitute the matter, and the light-matter interactions. The driven harmonic oscillator model expresses these elements in terms of Newton&rsquo;s equation of motion \(F = ma\) and forms a steppingstone toward more detailed treatments of light-matter interactions.</p>

<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/06:_Classical_Description_of_Spectroscopy/6.02:_Absorption_by_a_Driven_Oscillator" class="mt-section" id="section_1"><span id="Charged_particle_in_a_harmonic_potential"></span><h2 class="editable"><br />
Charged particle in a harmonic potential</h2>

<p>Let&rsquo;s start by discussing the matter. While a static electric field (\(\mathcal{E}_0\)) can exert a force on any charge \(q\), i.e. \(F&nbsp; =&nbsp; q\mathcal{E}_0\), the efficient transfer of energy between matter and oscillating electromagnetic radiation comes from resonance&mdash;the sustained periodic displacement of the charge that matches the driving electric field. The key requirement for resonance is that the charged particles are bound. The many electrons and nuclei in molecules feel a restoring force when a field pushes them away from their equilibrium configuration, whether that is the balance of the attractive and repulsive forces that govern the positions of nuclei or the distribution of electrons about the center of mass of a molecule. Even in magnetic resonance experiments, the fixed magnetic field of the spectrometer imposes a restoring force that aligns spins to be interrogated with resonant magnetic fields.</p>

<figure><a id="Fig+1%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" name="Fig+1%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator"></a><img alt="Graph displaying two parabolas: one blue and one red, both opening upwards, with their vertices aligned at the bottom." class="internal default" style="width: 500px; height: 290px;" width="500px" height="290px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/475318/Figure6-2-1.png?revision=3&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=500&amp;height=290" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{1}\): Harmonic approximation to a bound potential for small displacements from the equilibrium position \(x_0\).</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The simplest way of representing this problem is to consider the behavior of a single charged particle of mass \(m\) with charge \(q\) held within a harmonic potential&nbsp;<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Fig+1:+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" rel="internal">Figure&nbsp;6.2.1</a>&mdash;often called the Lorentz model or the electron-on-a-spring model. However, this model is just as applicable to a molecule with multiple bound charges that has an equilibrium configuration \(R_0\)&mdash;for instance, the bond length of a diatomic molecule. Generally, the potential energy of a bound particle (or molecule) \(V ( R )\) will have a minimum at \(R_0\). For small variations in the position of the particle near \(R_0\), we can perform an expansion of \(V( R )\) about \(R_0\). The leading term takes on a parabolic form:</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+1" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+1"></a>\[V(R) \approx \frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{\partial ^2V}{\partial R^2} \right)_{\! R = R_0} \! \! \! \! \left( R - R_0 \right)^2 %eq01\]</p>

<p>This is just the potential energy for a harmonic oscillator. Therefore, we describe the matter in our model using the harmonic potential</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+2" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+2"></a>\[V = \frac{1}{2}\kappa Q^2 %eq02\]</p>

<p>where the displacement coordinate \(Q = R - R_0\). The force constant \(\kappa \) is determined by the curvature of the potential near the minimum</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+3" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+3"></a>\[\kappa&nbsp; = \left( \frac{\partial ^2V}{\partial R^2} \right)_{R = R_0} %eq03\]</p>

<p>When the charged particle is displaced from equilibrium, the restoring force is</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+4" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+4"></a>\[F_{\text{res}} =&nbsp; - \frac{\partial V}{\partial Q} =&nbsp; - \kappa Q %eq04\]</p>

<p>This is the classic linear restoring force expression expected from Hooke&rsquo;s Law.</p>

<p>Without any further input into the model, Newton&rsquo;s equation of motion is</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+5" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+5"></a>\[F_{\text{res}} = ma %eq05\]</p>

<p>or</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+6" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+6"></a>\[m\frac{\partial ^2Q}{\partial t^2} + \kappa Q = 0 %eq06\]</p>

<p>To solve this equation we apply the boundary conditions, setting the position \(Q_0\) and velocity \(V_0\) of the particle at time \(t = 0\), and we obtain the following solution</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+7" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+7"></a>\[Q( t ) = Q_0&nbsp;\cos ( \omega _0t ) + \frac{V_0}{m\omega _0} \, \sin (\omega _0t) %eq07\]</p>

<p>As illustrated in <a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Fig+2:+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" rel="internal">Figure&nbsp;6.2.2</a>, the particle displays simple oscillatory motion at the natural resonance frequency \(\omega _0\)</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+8" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+8"></a>\[\omega _0 = \sqrt \frac{\kappa }{m} %eq08\]</p>

<p>The important observation is that a displaced particle oscillates at a frequency governed by the shape of the binding potential near its minimum.</p>

<p>As a next step, we also add a damping force to describe the flow of energy from the oscillator into its environment. This is added in part to broaden the model&rsquo;s description of the matter, but it is also crucial for describing the energy flow from the light to the matter. Even if a molecule is absorbing light in isolation, the accelerating charges will radiate an electric field, thereby emitting energy that damps the amplitude of its oscillations. The simplest way to incorporate this is to recognize that damping changes the amplitude of vibration with time and is therefore proportional to the velocity of the particle (\(V = \dot Q\)). We assign a constant \(b\), the friction coefficient, to describe the linear relation between the damping force and velocity: \(F_{\text{damp}} =&nbsp; - b\dot Q\).</p>

<figure><a id="Fig+2%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" name="Fig+2%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator"></a><img alt="A red sine wave graph with peaks and troughs, depicting an oscillating function along a horizontal axis." class="internal default" style="width: 451px; height: 275px;" width="451px" height="275px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/475317/Figure6-2-2.png?revision=3&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=451&amp;height=275" />
<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{2}\): Time-dependence of a particle in a harmonic potential in the absence of an external force, exhibiting oscillations with a period \(\tau = \nu _0^{-1}\)&nbsp; for a particle released from an initially displaced position \(Q(0) =Q_0\) with \(V_0=0\).</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>With damping, Newton&rsquo;s second law becomes</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+9" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+9"></a>\[\begin{aligned}<br />
&nbsp; F_{\text{res}} + F_{\text{damp}} &amp;= ma \\<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; - \kappa Q - b\frac{\partial Q}{\partial t} &amp;= m\frac{\partial ^2Q}{\partial t^2} \\<br />
\end{aligned} %eq09\]</p>

<p>Let&rsquo;s re-write this equation in a manner that will simplify the notation and interpretation going forward:</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+10" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+10"></a>\[\ddot Q + 2\gamma \dot Q + \omega _0^2Q = 0 %eq10\]</p>

<p>Here the damping constant \(\gamma \) is defined as</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+11" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+11"></a>\[\gamma&nbsp; = \frac{b}{2m} %eq11\]</p>

<p>For the second-order homogeneous equation such as eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+10" rel="internal">6.2.10</a>), we find oscillatory solutions similar to eq.&nbsp;(<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+7" rel="internal">6.2.7</a>) with modifications due to damping:</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+12" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+12"></a>\[Q(t) = e^{- \gamma t}&nbsp;\left[A\cos \Omega _0t + B\sin \Omega _0t&nbsp;\right] %eq12\]</p>

<p>with \(A = Q_0\) and \(B = \left( \gamma Q_0 + V_0 \right) \Omega_0^{- 1}\). As example of this solution is illustrated in <a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Fig+3:+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" rel="internal">Figure&nbsp;6.2.3</a>. The damping force has modified the position equation in two ways. First is the exponential decrease of the oscillation amplitude over time from \(e^ {- \gamma t}\). Second, the particle oscillates at a reduced frequency:</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+13" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+13"></a>\[\Omega _0 = \sqrt {\omega _0^2 - \gamma ^2}&nbsp; %eq13\]</p>

<figure><a id="Fig+3%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" name="Fig+3%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator"></a><img alt="Graph showing two curves: a blue line representing an exponential decay and a red line with oscillations over time." class="internal default" style="width: 450px; height: 281px;" width="450px" height="281px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/475311/Figure6-2-3.png?revision=4&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=450&amp;height=281" />
<figcaption>
<p>Figure \(\PageIndex{3}\): Example of the response of a damped oscillator initially positioned at \(Q=0\)<span style="color:#1F497D;mso-themecolor:text2;mso-bidi-font-weight:bold;"><span style="position:relative;top:4.0pt;mso-text-raise:-4.0pt;"><shapetype coordsize="21600,21600" filled="f" id="_x0000_t75" path="m@4@5l@4@11@9@11@9@5xe" preferrelative="t" spt="75" stroked="f"> <stroke joinstyle="miter"> <formulas> <f eqn="if lineDrawn pixelLineWidth 0"> <f eqn="sum @0 1 0"> <f eqn="sum 0 0 @1"> <f eqn="prod @2 1 2"> <f eqn="prod @3 21600 pixelWidth"> <f eqn="prod @3 21600 pixelHeight"> <f eqn="sum @0 0 1"> <f eqn="prod @6 1 2"> <f eqn="prod @7 21600 pixelWidth"> <f eqn="sum @8 21600 0"> <f eqn="prod @7 21600 pixelHeight"> <f eqn="sum @10 21600 0"> </f></f></f></f></f></f></f></f></f></f></f></f></formulas> <path connecttype="rect" extrusionok="f" gradientshapeok="t"> <lock aspectratio="t" ext="edit"> </lock></path></stroke></shapetype><shape id="_x0000_i1025" ole="" style="width:24pt;
 height:13pt" type="#_x0000_t75"> <imagedata src="file:///C:/Users/alichang/AppData/Local/Temp/msohtmlclip1/01/clip_image001.wmz" title=""> </imagedata></shape></span></span>with a positive velocity \(V_0\).</p>
</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The strength of the damping (\(\gamma \)) modifies the natural frequency of a bound particle from the value predicted by the curvature of the binding potential (\(\omega _0\)), resulting in the reduced frequency \(\Omega _0\). However, the most common case in molecular spectroscopy is the underdamped oscillator, which occurs when the damping constant \(\gamma \) is much smaller than \(\omega _0\). Therefore, the reduced frequency of an underdamped oscillator is \(\Omega _0 \approx \omega _0\). In this limit we can simply substitute \(\omega _0\) for \(\Omega _0\) in eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+12" rel="internal">6.2.12</a>) without any serious concern.</p>

<p>In the opposite limit of strong damping, \(\gamma&nbsp; &gt; \omega _0\), the oscillator&rsquo;s kinetic energy dissipates in a shorter time than the oscillatory period, and \(\Omega _0\) takes on imaginary values. However, we don&rsquo;t need to be concerned about an unphysical, imaginary oscillation because the constant \(B\) also becomes imaginary, ensuring the ultimate solution is real.&nbsp; Using \(\cos ( b\mathrm{i}) = \cosh b\) and \(\sin (b\mathrm{i}) = \mathrm{i}&nbsp;\sinh b\), we can show that what was an oscillatory function in the underdamped regime becomes an exponentially damped function in the overdamped regime. That is, the term \(\sin \Omega _0t\) becomes \(\sinh ( \Gamma t&nbsp;)\), where the damping rate \(\Gamma\) is</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+14" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+14"></a>\[\Gamma&nbsp; = \sqrt {\gamma ^2 - \omega _0^2}&nbsp; %eq14\]</p>

<p>The general solution in the overdamped limit can be written as a sum of damped exponentials</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+15" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+15"></a>\[\begin{aligned}<br />
&nbsp; Q(t) &amp;= e^{- \gamma t} \left[ A\cosh (\Gamma t) + B\sinh (\Gamma t) \right] \\<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &amp;= \frac{1}{2} ( A + B) e^{- (\gamma&nbsp; - \Gamma )t } + \frac{1}{2} ( A - B)&nbsp;e^{ - (\gamma&nbsp; + \Gamma )t} \\<br />
\end{aligned}&nbsp; %eq15\]</p>

<section class="box-note">
<div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/06:_Classical_Description_of_Spectroscopy/6.02:_Absorption_by_a_Driven_Oscillator" class="mt-section"><span id="A_math_digression"></span><h5 class="box-legend editable"><span class="lt-icon-default">A math digression</span> &nbsp;</h5>

<p>At this point, it is worth noting that the solutions to equations of the form of eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+10" rel="internal">6.2.10</a>) can be written multiple ways. Remember, the general solution to homogeneous linear second order differential equations can be expressed in terms of the roots to the characteristic equation \(ar^2 + br + c = 0\), leading to \(Q(t) = c_1e^{r_1t }+ c_2e^{r_2t}\), where \(r_1\) and \(r_2\) are complex numbers.&nbsp; In the present case, we know that \(\kappa \), \(m\), and \(b\) are real, so we only have real valued solutions, resulting in forms like eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+12" rel="internal">6.2.12</a>). &nbsp;</p>

<p>Now, let&rsquo;s consider the form of eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+7" rel="internal">6.2.7</a>), and by extension eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+12" rel="internal">6.2.12</a>), using the constants \(A\) and \(B\):</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+16" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+16"></a>\[Q(t) = A ( \omega _0t ) + B\cos ( \omega _0t ) %eq16\]</p>

<p>This consists of two elements, sine and cosine, which are oscillating with the same frequency but each phase-shifted \(\text{90}^\circ\)&nbsp;from the other. The cosine is referred to as &ldquo;in-phase,&rdquo; while sine is &ldquo;in-quadrature.&rdquo; Signals which are in-quadrature can be re-written in terms of a single amplitude and phase using the following relationships:</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+17" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+17"></a>\[\begin{aligned}<br />
A \sin \omega _0t&nbsp; \pm&nbsp; B\cos \omega _0t&nbsp; &amp;= \sqrt {A^2 + B^2} \, \sin (\omega _0t&nbsp; \pm \beta ') &amp; &amp; \beta'= \operatorname{atan} (B/A) \\<br />
&amp;= \sqrt {A^2 + B^2 \vphantom{|}} \, \cos (\omega _0t&nbsp; \pm \beta ) &amp; &amp; \beta = \operatorname{atan} (A/B)<br />
\end{aligned}&nbsp;%eq17\]</p>

<p>For instance, we can use the identities in eqn. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+17" rel="internal">6.2.17</a>) to write eqn. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+12" rel="internal">6.2.12</a>) in the form</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+18" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+18"></a>\[Q(t) = \sqrt {A^2 + B^2 \vphantom{|}}&nbsp; e^{ - \gamma t}\cos (\Omega _0t&nbsp; \pm \beta ) %eq18\]</p>

<p>To convert the amplitude (\(\sqrt {A^2 + B^2} \)) and phase \((\beta \)) back to the in-quadrature components, we can use the identities</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+19" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+19"></a>\[\begin{aligned}<br />
&nbsp; \cos (\omega _0t&nbsp; \pm \beta ) &amp;= \cos (\beta )\cos (\omega _0t) \, \mp \, \sin (\beta )\sin (\omega _0t) \\[3pt]<br />
&nbsp; \sin (\omega _0t&nbsp; \pm \beta ) &amp;= \sin (\beta )\cos (\omega _0t)&nbsp; \, \pm&nbsp; \, \cos (\beta )\sin (\omega _0t) \\<br />
\end{aligned}&nbsp; %eq19\]</p>

<p>Here are a few additional trigonometric identities that prove useful in describing driven harmonic oscillator problems:</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+20" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+20"></a>\[\begin{aligned}<br />
&nbsp; \cos \left( a + b\mathrm{i}&nbsp;\right) &amp;= \cos a\cosh b - \mathrm{i}\sin a \sinh b \\<br />
&nbsp; \sin (a + b\mathrm{i}) &amp;= \sin a\cosh b + \mathrm{i}\cos a\sinh b \\<br />
&nbsp; \cos \left( b\mathrm{i}&nbsp;\right) &amp;= \cosh b \\<br />
&nbsp; \sin (b\mathrm{i}) &amp;= \mathrm{i}\sinh b \\<br />
\end{aligned}&nbsp; %eq20\]</p>
</div></section>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/06:_Classical_Description_of_Spectroscopy/6.02:_Absorption_by_a_Driven_Oscillator" class="mt-section" id="section_2"><span id="Driving_the_oscillator_sinusoidally"></span><h2 class="editable"><br />
Driving the oscillator sinusoidally</h2>

<p>Now let&rsquo;s turn to discussing the light. Light is a traveling electromagnetic field that oscillates in time and in space and is characterized by several factors. However, in the present case, our interest is limited to the sinusoidally oscillating force that it exerts on the matter, and therefore we drop spatial variables and phase. We retain only the essential variables: the oscillating frequency \(\omega \) and the amplitude of the electric field, \(\mathcal{E}_0\).</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+21" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+21"></a>\[\mathcal{E}(t) = \mathcal{E}_0\cos&nbsp;( \omega t&nbsp;) %eq21\]</p>

<figure>
<p><a id="Fig+4%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" name="Fig+4%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator"></a><img alt="A red sinusoidal wave graph showing peaks and troughs along a horizontal axis, labeled with values." class="internal default" style="width: 400px; height: 295px;" width="400px" height="295px" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/475319/Figure6-2-4.png?revision=3&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=400&amp;height=295" /></p>

<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{4}\): Sinusoidal driving field with an amplitude \(\mathcal{E}_0\) and angular frequency \(\omega\).</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Next, how does this field interact with the molecule&rsquo;s charges? We take the distribution of charge within the molecule to be defined by the dipole moment \(\mu \) and the interaction potential of the field and the dipole is \(V(t) =&nbsp; - \mu&nbsp; \cdot \mathcal{E}(t)\). For the case of a single particle of charge \(q\), one can express the dipole moment as the charge times the displacement of the oscillator, \(\mu&nbsp; = qQ\); however, this expression can also be extended to molecules with a sum over charges. The external driving force that the field exerts on the oscillator can then be calculated from \(F_{\text{ext}} =&nbsp; - \partial V/\partial Q\), which leads to a sinusoidal form with an amplitude \(F_0\) and a driving frequency \(\omega _0\).</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+22" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+22"></a>\[F_{\text{ext}}(t) = F_0\cos&nbsp;( \omega t ) %eq22\]</p>

<p>Here, \(F_0\) is the amplitude of the driving force. For a single charged particle, it would be \(F_0 = q\mathcal{E}_0\), but for a molecule in three dimensions, we express it as</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+23" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+23"></a>\[\begin{align}\begin{aligned}<br />
&nbsp; F_0 &amp;= \left( \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu }}{\partial Q} \right) \cdot \boldsymbol{\mathcal{E}}_0 \\<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &amp;\equiv \boldsymbol{\mu}' \cdot \boldsymbol{\mathcal{E}}_0 \\<br />
\end{aligned}\end{align}&nbsp; %eq23\]</p>

<p>In this expression, the dipole derivative \(\boldsymbol{\mu}' = \left( \partial \boldsymbol{\mu }/\partial Q \right)\) reflects the fact that the light&rsquo;s forces redistribute the charges within the molecule and alter the dipole moment. That is, the field polarizes the molecule. This factor is referred to as the transition dipole moment in vibrational and electronic spectroscopy or the polarizability when discussing general distortions of electron density by electric fields. Specifically, eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+23" rel="internal">6.2.23</a>) indicates that two factors influence the electric field&rsquo;s force on the oscillator: (1) the charge displacement is proportional to the magnitude of the field, and (2) efficient interactions require alignment between the electric field polarization and the transition dipole moment because there is a dot product between the direction of charge displacement and the field. We&rsquo;ll drop the vector notation now for simplicity.</p>

<p>Adding this third force term to eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+9" rel="internal">6.2.9</a>) results in the driven oscillator equation</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+24" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+24"></a>\[\ddot Q + 2\gamma \dot Q + \omega _0^2Q = \frac{F_0}{m}\cos \left( \omega t \right) %eq24\]</p>

<p>As a result of adding a time-dependent force to eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+10" rel="internal">6.2.10</a>), this equation is an inhomogeneous second-order ODE. The general solution, <a title="6.6: Appendix- Laplace Transform Solution for the Driven Damped Oscillator" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/06%3A_Classical_Description_of_Spectroscopy/6.06%3A_New_Page" rel="internal">described in an appendix</a>, can be expressed as the sum of a steady-state solution and a transient solution. The steady-state solution is only determined by the form of the driving force, while the transient solution is determined by the initial conditions, i.e., the position and velocity of the particle at \(t = 0\).</p>

<p>Our calculation of the absorption spectrum comes from the steady state solution to eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+24" rel="internal">6.2.24</a>), which assumes that the oscillator has come to equilibrium, when the continuous energy input from the electric field is in balance with the dissipation of energy caused by damping. The steady-state solution takes the form</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+25" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+25"></a>\[Q(t) = Q_0\cos&nbsp; ( \omega t - \beta&nbsp;) %eq25\]</p>

<p>where the amplitude of the driven oscillator is</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+26" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+26"></a>\[Q_0 = \frac{F_0}{m \sqrt {\left( \omega _0^2 - \omega ^2 \right)^2 + 4\gamma^2 \omega ^2} } %eq26\]</p>

<p>and the oscillator&rsquo;s phase is given by</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+27" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+27"></a>\[\tan \beta&nbsp; = \frac{2\gamma \omega }{\omega _0^2 - \omega ^2} %eq27\]</p>

<p>Let&rsquo;s look at the behavior of this oscillator, starting with the maximum displacement \(Q_0\). Equation (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+26" rel="internal">6.2.26</a>) shows that displacement is proportional to the magnitude of the light&rsquo;s driving force, but it also has a so-called &ldquo;resonant denominator&rdquo; that is dependent on the oscillator&rsquo;s frequency and damping constant. The highest displacements are realized on resonance, when the frequency of the driving field matches the natural frequency of the system (\(\omega _0 = \omega \)). One can still drive the particle off resonance, but the degree to which one can displace the particle falls off with the detuning (\(\omega&nbsp; - \omega _0\)) in a manner that depends on the damping. Damping always decreases \(Q_0\) relative to the undamped oscillator, but this effect is most pronounced near resonance. These features are illustrated in <a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Fig+5:+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" rel="internal">Figure&nbsp;6.2.5a</a>.</p>

<figure>
<p><a id="Fig+5%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" name="Fig+5%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator"></a><img alt="Three graphs arranged horizontally: the first shows a peak, the second features oscillating lines, and the third displays cumulative curves." class="internal default" loading="lazy" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/475320/Figure6-2-5.png?revision=3" /></p>

<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{5}\): Steady-state solution to the sinusoidally driven underdamped harmonic oscillator. (a) Maximum displacement of the oscillator \(Q_0\) as a function of detuning from resonance, \(\omega = \omega_0\), and damping coefficient, \(\gamma\).&nbsp; Note the asymmetry in the lineshape with a finite value of \(Q_0\) at \(\omega = 0\). (b) Illustration of the phase-shift \(\beta\) in the time-dependent oscillations of \(Q(t)\) relative to the driving field \(\mathcal{E}(t)\). (c) Magnitude of the oscillator phase relative to the driving field as a function of detuning. Different lines correspond to the different damping coefficients in (a).</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>From eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+25" rel="internal">6.2.25</a>), notice that the particle oscillates at the driving frequency \(\omega \) and not at the resonance frequency \(\omega _0\), but with a phase shift \(\beta \) relative to the driving field. On resonance \(\beta&nbsp; = \pi /2\), and the particle oscillates as \(\sin \omega t\), lagging behind the phase of the field by \(\text{90}^\circ\)&nbsp;<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Fig+5:+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" rel="internal">Figure&nbsp;6.2.5b</a>. The maximum force is exerted on the particle at \(Q = 0\), the position where the velocity and kinetic energy of the particle are at maximum. The external force drops to zero when the particle reaches its classical turning points (\( \pm Q_0\)). The phase shift \(\beta \) depends on the detuning from resonance, varying from \(0\) to \(\pi \) with increasing \(\omega \) <a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Fig+5:+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" rel="internal">Figure&nbsp;6.2.5c</a>. When \(\omega&nbsp; \ll \omega _0\), \(\beta&nbsp; = 0\), so the particle is in-phase with the driving field. This means that the field changes slowly, and the particle position adiabatically tracks the oscillations of the field.</p>

<p>Using eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+17" rel="internal">6.2.17</a>) we can re-write eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+25" rel="internal">6.2.25</a>) in terms of a cosine component that is in-phase with the driving field and a sine component that is in-quadrature (\(\pi /2\) phase-shifted)</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+28" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+28"></a>\[ Q (t) = Q_0 \left[ \frac{2\gamma \omega }{\sqrt {\left( \omega _0^2 - \omega ^2 \right)^2 + \left( 2\gamma \omega&nbsp; \right)^2 }} \, \sin ( \omega t ) + \frac{\left( \omega _0^2 - \omega ^2 \right)}{\sqrt {\left( \omega _0^2 - \omega ^2 \right)^2 + \left( 2\gamma \omega&nbsp; \right)^2 }} \,&nbsp;\cos ( \omega t )<br />
\right] %eq28 \]</p>

<p>This shows that the maximum amplitude of oscillation for a given driving frequency is described by the leading term in this equation.</p>

<p>Note that the driving field is harmonically displacing the charged particle, and therefore the external field is inducing an oscillating dipole moment of the form \(\mu (t) = \mu 'Q(t)\). As we will see next, the sine component is proportional to the power absorbed by the field and is referred to as the absorptive part of the oscillator response, whereas the cosine term in eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+28" rel="internal">6.2.28</a>) is the dispersive part that encodes the phase shift of the matter with respect to the field.</p>

</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/06:_Classical_Description_of_Spectroscopy/6.02:_Absorption_by_a_Driven_Oscillator" class="mt-section" id="section_3"><span id="Absorption_Spectrum"></span><h2 class="editable"><br />
Absorption Spectrum</h2>

<p>The absorption spectrum indicates the amount of power transferred from the light to the oscillator as a function of the incident frequency of the light, which we can calculate from the average value of the force acting on the oscillator times the velocity of the particle. The power can then be decomposed into the average force acting on an oscillator times the particle velocity. For a continuously oscillating field, the average power absorbed is obtained by averaging the steady state solution over one cycle of the field.</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+29" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+29"></a>\[\begin{aligned}<br />
&nbsp; \overline {\mathcal{P}} _{\text{abs}}<br />
&nbsp; &amp;= \overline{F \cdot \dot Q} &nbsp;<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; = \frac{2\pi }{\omega } \int_0^{2\pi /\omega}&nbsp; F(t) \cdot \dot Q(t)&nbsp;\, dt&nbsp; \\<br />
&nbsp;&nbsp; &amp;= \frac{\gamma \omega ^2F_0^2}{m}\frac{1}{ \left( \omega ^2 - \omega _0^2 \right)^2 + 4 \omega ^2 \gamma ^2}<br />
\end{aligned}&nbsp; %eq29\]</p>

<p>One observation that we can make immediately is that power absorption from the field requires \(\gamma&nbsp; &gt; 0\). That is, if there is no damping&mdash;no dissipation of energy by the oscillator&mdash;the energy in the driving field is not irreversibly absorbed by the oscillator but is instead transferred back and forth between the coupled oscillator and field at steady-state. From eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+23" rel="internal">6.2.23</a>) we also see that the absorption of energy from the field is proportional to the square of the dipole derivative and the intensity of the electric field: \(F_0^2 = \left( \boldsymbol{\mu}' \right)^2 \boldsymbol{ \mathcal{E}}_0^2\). Finally, note that the absorbed power is proportional to the sine term in eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+28" rel="internal">6.2.28</a>), which is why the sine component is often referred to as the absorptive part of the oscillator response.</p>

<p>For most optical and infrared spectroscopy, resonances are observed to be in the weak damping limit, \(\gamma&nbsp; \ll \omega _0\). Under these circumstances, it is reasonable to describe the absorption spectrum by making a near-resonance approximation where \(\omega _0 \approx \omega \), and we can simplify the denominator in eq. (<a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+29" rel="internal">6.2.29</a>) as</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+30" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+30"></a>\[\left( \omega _0^2 - \omega ^2 \right) = \left( \omega _0 + \omega&nbsp; \right)\left( \omega _0 - \omega&nbsp; \right) \approx 2\omega \left( \omega _0 - \omega&nbsp; \right) %eq30\]</p>

<p>and re-write it as</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+31" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+31"></a>\[\overline {\mathcal{P}} _{\text{abs}} = \frac{\gamma }{2m}\left( \boldsymbol{\mu}' \right)^2 \! \boldsymbol{ \mathcal{E}}_0^2 \frac{1}{\left( \omega&nbsp; - \omega _0 \right)^2 + \gamma ^2} %eq31\]</p>

<p>This is known as a Lorentzian lineshape <a class="F1 mt-self-link" title="6.2: Absorption by a Driven Oscillator" href="#Fig+6:+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" rel="internal">Figure&nbsp;6.2.6</a>. It peaks at the resonance frequency, has a full width half-maximum (FWHM) line width of \(2\gamma \), and the integrated area is \(\pi(\mu')^2&nbsp;\!&nbsp;\mathcal{E}_0^2/4m\). This steady state model describes the behavior analyzed by frequency domain spectroscopy. In frequency domain spectroscopy, the power (or intensity) absorbed is measured as a function of a tuned monochromatic frequency. The resonances we find in the absorption data allow us to identify the molecular parameters \(\omega _0\), \(\gamma \), and \(\mu '\).</p>

<figure>
<p><a id="Fig+6%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator" name="Fig+6%3A+Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator"></a><img alt="Graph of a red curve with a sharply peaked center, resembling a bell shape, on a black background." class="internal default" loading="lazy" style="width: 350px; height: 344px;" width="350px" height="344px" src="https://chem.libretexts.org/@api/deki/files/475321/Figure6-2-6.png?revision=2&amp;size=bestfit&amp;width=350&amp;height=344" /></p>

<figcaption>Figure \(\PageIndex{6}\): Lorentzian lineshape illustrating the parameters that govern linewidth and intensity in the absorption spectrum.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>An experimental parameter commonly used to describe the strength of a resonance is the absorption cross-section, \(\sigma \). It can be obtained by measuring the power of light absorbed relative to the incident intensity. Using the formula for the intensity of the incident light \(I_0 = \tfrac{1}{2}\varepsilon _0c\mathcal{E}_0^2\), and remembering that \(\mu ' = {\partial \mu&nbsp; / \partial Q}\) we find</p>

<p><a id="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+32" name="Absorption+by+a+Driven+Oscillator+eq.+32"></a>\[\sigma (\omega )<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; = \frac{\overline{\mathcal{P}} _{\text{abs}}}{I_0}<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; = \underbrace {\frac{\pi }{mc\varepsilon_0}\left( \frac{\partial \mu }{\partial Q}\right)^{ \!\!\, 2}}_\text{magnitude}&nbsp;<br />
&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;\underbrace{\frac{\gamma /\pi }{\left( \omega&nbsp; - \omega _0 \right)^2 + \gamma ^2}}_{\begin{smallmatrix}&nbsp; \text{area normalized} \\ \text{lineshape}\end{smallmatrix}} %eq32\]</p>

<p>This equation is written such that the overall magnitude of the power that a molecular transition can absorb is spread across an area-normalized Lorentzian lineshape. The linewidth may vary based on interactions of the molecule with its environment. As discussed in <a title="7.6: Absorption Cross Section" href="https://chem.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Physical_and_Theoretical_Chemistry_Textbook_Maps/Time-Dependent_Quantum_Mechanics_and_Spectroscopy_2025e_(Tokmakoff)/07%3A_Interaction_of_Light_and_Matter/7.06%3A_Absorption_Cross_Section" rel="internal">Sec. 7.6.</a>, the cross-section is proportional to the commonly used extinction coefficient, which is determined from Beer&rsquo;s Law. While one can directly read the parameters \(\omega _0\) and \(\gamma \) from the peak position and line width of an absorption spectrum, converting the absorption spectrum into a cross section or extinction coefficient allows one to obtain a quantitative measure of \(\left( \partial \mu /\partial Q \right)^2\).</p>
</div>

